482
00:26:21,700 --> 00:26:24,740
here, here we see well-defined
P-waves as we mentioned

483
00:26:24,740 --> 00:26:28,800
before, but there are not
really any good P-waves

484
00:26:28,800 --> 00:26:32,140
here. What we see here are
known as virulatory waves,

485
00:26:32,300 --> 00:26:36,680
aka not true P
-waves. And as we can

486
00:26:36,680 --> 00:26:38,780
tell here, the
distances between one

487
00:26:38,780 --> 00:26:45,820
QRS complex to
another are varying,

488
00:26:46,340 --> 00:26:47,240
a.k.a.

489
00:26:47,360 --> 00:26:50,180
they're irregular,
meaning this is

490
00:26:50,180 --> 00:26:52,740
an irregularly
irregular rhythm.

491
00:26:52,820 --> 00:26:57,160
Again, in AFib, before
we go into it deeper in

492
00:26:57,160 --> 00:27:00,120
future lectures, there's
no distinct P waves,

493
00:27:00,120 --> 00:27:03,280
simply just random
squiggling back and forth.

494
00:27:03,480 --> 00:27:06,500
So in the bottom left,
you'll see something that's

495
00:27:06,500 --> 00:27:09,560
really tricky, almost like
normal sinus rhythm. So if

496
00:27:09,560 --> 00:27:14,060
you look closely, there are
P waves with no linked QRS

497
00:27:14,360 --> 00:27:19,640
complex immediately after
it. These P waves are not

498
00:27:19,640 --> 00:27:22,240
necessarily associated with
the QRS complex, but the

499
00:27:22,240 --> 00:27:25,340
rate is regular, as is the
P wave. This is because

500
00:27:25,340 --> 00:27:28,920
this represents when the atria
in particular beat without

501
00:27:28,920 --> 00:27:32,520
synchronization in what
is known as complete or

502
00:27:32,520 --> 00:27:37,340
third degree AV or heart
block, another abnormal rhythm

503
00:27:37,340 --> 00:27:40,640
that we'll get on and focus
on in a future lecture.

504
00:27:40,980 --> 00:27:44,400
And lastly, in the
bottom right-hand corner,

505
00:27:44,400 --> 00:27:46,820
you'll see a flat
line called asystole.

506
00:27:47,520 --> 00:27:50,100
If you see this and your
patient is looking fine,

507
00:27:50,100 --> 00:27:51,780
it's probably because
something's unplugged.

508
00:27:51,780 --> 00:27:54,280
But if you see this and
your patient's unresponsive,

509
00:27:54,280 --> 00:27:56,760
that's because they're
in cardiac arrest,

510
00:27:56,760 --> 00:28:00,340
aka dead, and need
immediate resuscitation.

511
00:28:01,180 --> 00:28:03,920
All in all, I wouldn't again
focus too much about the

512
00:28:03,920 --> 00:28:06,620
specifics of what we
mentioned, atrial fibrillation,

513
00:28:06,620 --> 00:28:10,340
AV blocks, etc., as we'll
discuss this in a later

514
00:28:10,340 --> 00:28:13,620
lecture. What's very
important now is that you can

515
00:28:13,620 --> 00:28:17,140
identify normal sinus rhythm,
that every P wave precedes

516
00:28:17,140 --> 00:28:21,200
a QRS complex and every
P wave has a QRS complex.

517
00:28:22,680 --> 00:28:27,380
So after you determine if
the rhythm is or is not

518
00:28:27,380 --> 00:28:30,120
sinus rhythm, it's time to
determine if your rhythm

519
00:28:30,120 --> 00:28:33,520
is regular or irregular.
So many students combine

520
00:28:33,520 --> 00:28:36,820
these two steps with more
practice. On the left,

521
00:28:36,820 --> 00:28:39,190
you can see a regular
rhythm. If you took a line

522
00:28:39,190 --> 00:28:42,860
piece of paper or calipers
and noted all the R

523
00:28:42,860 --> 00:28:45,640
waves, they would be
equidistant from each other.

524
00:28:46,120 --> 00:28:49,320
On the right is an
irregular rhythm. You can

525
00:28:49,320 --> 00:28:52,380
see immediately the R
waves are not equidistant

526
00:28:52,380 --> 00:28:55,360
from one another.
For harder cases,

527
00:28:55,360 --> 00:28:58,260
using the paper method
or calipers would help

528
00:28:58,260 --> 00:29:01,680
you define or identify
an irregular rhythm.

529
00:29:02,220 --> 00:29:05,260
So next, step number
four, determine

530
00:29:05,260 --> 00:29:08,500
the axis, or always
in our mnemonic.

531
00:29:08,730 --> 00:29:11,400
So sorry to trigger
you guys again, but

532
00:29:11,400 --> 00:29:13,680
we're back to axis from
our previous lecture.

533
00:29:13,780 --> 00:29:17,100
So remember that the axis
is the major direction

534
00:29:17,100 --> 00:29:20,200
of electrical activity
flowing through the heart.

535
00:29:20,200 --> 00:29:24,080
If the heart is hypertrophied
in any location, this may

536
00:29:24,080 --> 00:29:26,560
cause the signal to travel
farther and change the

537
00:29:26,560 --> 00:29:30,880
direction of the vector. The
most efficient way to estimate

538
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:35,120
or look at the axis is
comparing the direction or

539
00:29:35,120 --> 00:29:40,660
orientation of the QRS
complexes in lead 1 and AVF. As

540
00:29:40,660 --> 00:29:43,400
you mentioned prior, if
we go back to Eindhoven's

541
00:29:43,400 --> 00:29:47,460
triangle, we saw that leads 1
and AVF were perpendicular to

542
00:29:47,460 --> 00:29:49,960
each other, meaning those
are the best two leads to

543
00:29:49,960 --> 00:29:53,120
use to compare. This is also
the preferred method that

544
00:29:53,120 --> 00:29:57,320
Dr. Cohen likes to use and
a method that I continue to

545
00:29:57,320 --> 00:30:01,940
use. So now continuing along
with the open triangle as

546
00:30:01,940 --> 00:30:04,480
we mentioned in the prior
slide in previous lecture.

547
00:30:05,100 --> 00:30:08,500
Positive inflection means
that we have depolarization

548
00:30:08,500 --> 00:30:11,520
that occurs towards
a lead. Now as you

549
00:30:11,520 --> 00:30:14,680
mentioned just before the
two leads that we use are

550
00:30:14,970 --> 00:30:19,000
right leads one in AVF
one determining the axis.

551
00:30:19,060 --> 00:30:22,140
So we spoke in great
detail about vectors, but

552
00:30:22,140 --> 00:30:24,520
here we're going to use
an easier method that

553
00:30:24,520 --> 00:30:27,020
will help you on your
clinical rotations to quickly

554
00:30:27,020 --> 00:30:29,780
identify if your axis
is normal or abnormal.

555
00:30:30,320 --> 00:30:33,380
The method that we want to
use is called the thumbs

556
00:30:33,380 --> 00:30:36,580
up method. So if your
left hand represents

557
00:30:36,580 --> 00:30:40,680
lead one and your right
hand represents lead AVF,

558
00:30:41,020 --> 00:30:44,440
you can determine the
axis. You can even pull out

559
00:30:44,440 --> 00:30:46,620
your thumbs right now,
practice alone in your

560
00:30:46,620 --> 00:30:49,080
cubicle with a loved one
or a random person on the

561
00:30:49,080 --> 00:30:51,300
street. If you really
want to hammer this home.

562
00:30:51,880 --> 00:30:56,780
So thumbs up refers to
a positive QRS complex.

563
00:30:56,940 --> 00:31:01,040
Thumbs down refers to a
negative QRS complex. If your

564
00:31:01,040 --> 00:31:03,940
ECG comes to you, you've
already oriented yourself,

565
00:31:03,940 --> 00:31:07,680
have your rate rhythm,
you could drop the EKG and

566
00:31:07,680 --> 00:31:11,580
put your thumbs out. So if
both of your thumbs go up,

567
00:31:11,840 --> 00:31:14,420
this means that there's a
positive inflection of the

568
00:31:14,420 --> 00:31:18,300
QRS complex and leads one
in AVF, meaning you have a

569
00:31:18,300 --> 00:31:22,000
normal axis. If both hands
are down, meaning you have

570
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:26,060
a negative inflection of
the QRS complex in leads 1

571
00:31:26,060 --> 00:31:28,920
and AVF, this means you
have an extreme axis. This

572
00:31:28,920 --> 00:31:31,620
doesn't happen too often, to
be honest. You'll most likely

573
00:31:31,620 --> 00:31:36,000
encounter left or rightward
axis deviations, meaning

574
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:39,700
in a leftward axis deviation,
you have your thumb

575
00:31:39,700 --> 00:31:44,100
pointing upward, your left
hand in lead 1, and your

576
00:31:44,100 --> 00:31:46,900
thumb on your right hand
pointing downward in lead AVF

577
00:31:46,900 --> 00:31:50,880
and the opposite occurring
for right axis deviation.

578
00:31:50,900 --> 00:31:54,240
Your left thumb pointing
downward for lead 1 and

579
00:31:54,240 --> 00:31:57,360
your right thumb pointing
upward for lead AVF.

580
00:31:58,300 --> 00:32:01,960
So there are a bunch of
causes of axis deviation.

581
00:32:02,370 --> 00:32:05,000
Some of the high yield
ones are listed here.

582
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:07,000
For board's sake though,
I would definitely

583
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:09,280
know left ventricular
hypertrophy causes left

584
00:32:09,280 --> 00:32:12,780
axis deviation and
how to identify it. it

585
00:32:12,780 --> 00:32:14,880
was one that actually
came up on my comlex.

586
00:32:17,480 --> 00:32:20,020
So I won't include questions
here because of the

587
00:32:20,020 --> 00:32:22,960
ending where we'll be
reviewing two ECGs together.

588
00:32:22,960 --> 00:32:26,540
This encompasses both
objective two and three.

589
00:32:30,200 --> 00:32:35,200
So now step five, we're
on intervals or impresses

590
00:32:35,200 --> 00:32:38,540
in our mnemonic. So
the PR interval normal

591
00:32:38,540 --> 00:32:42,140
duration is between 120
to 200 milliseconds.

592
00:32:42,400 --> 00:32:46,140
Longer than one big box would
therefore be a prolonged

593
00:32:46,140 --> 00:32:49,400
PR interval. Remember,
this is the time it takes

594
00:32:49,400 --> 00:32:52,840
to get from the SA through
the AV node. So for this

595
00:32:52,840 --> 00:32:57,200
reason, PR interval is crucial
in identifying AV blocks.

596
00:32:57,200 --> 00:32:59,260
It's an important measure
that you should definitely

597
00:32:59,260 --> 00:33:02,000
know for clinical practice
and for board exams.

598
00:33:09,350 --> 00:33:11,830
So the normal QRS
duration is less

599
00:33:11,830 --> 00:33:14,770
than 120 milliseconds,
or three boxes.

600
00:33:14,910 --> 00:33:17,390
The way I like to
remember this is there are

601
00:33:17,390 --> 00:33:20,670
three letters in QRS, so
I know that the normal

602
00:33:20,670 --> 00:33:24,510
duration of a QRS complex
is three small boxes.

603
00:33:24,810 --> 00:33:27,870
Now, a widened QRS
complex has many causes,

604
00:33:27,870 --> 00:33:30,050
as we discussed in
the previous lectures,

605
00:33:30,050 --> 00:33:32,480
such as bundle branch
block, hyperkalemia,

606
00:33:32,750 --> 00:33:36,090
tricyclic or TCAs, Wolf
-Parkinson-White syndrome,

607
00:33:36,090 --> 00:33:39,600
ventricular pacing, and
hypothermia. They're all tied to

608
00:33:39,600 --> 00:33:41,910
physiology, so feel free to
go back and review any of

609
00:33:41,910 --> 00:33:44,410
these pathologies or email
me to discuss them further.

610
00:33:50,280 --> 00:33:54,960
Now, a prolonged QT interval
is very important to be

611
00:33:54,960 --> 00:33:57,860
able to identify because
a prolonged QT interval

612
00:33:57,860 --> 00:34:01,580
increases the risk of dangerous
arrhythmias, particularly

613
00:34:01,580 --> 00:34:04,320
ventricular arrhythmias
like torsionis de point,

614
00:34:04,380 --> 00:34:07,880
which can then degenerate
into lethal arrhythmias

615
00:34:07,880 --> 00:34:11,160
such as ventricular
fibrillation. Now, a good

616
00:34:11,160 --> 00:34:13,840
rule of thumb is that a
normal QT is less than half

617
00:34:13,840 --> 00:34:17,880
of the R to R ratio. So
if we were to go between

618
00:34:18,160 --> 00:34:24,640
two R waves, so we have
our, excuse me, our P wave,

619
00:34:25,550 --> 00:34:29,540
Q, R, S, all by our T wave,

620
00:34:29,540 --> 00:34:32,240
and then again, our P wave,

621
00:34:32,660 --> 00:34:35,560
Q, R, S, T wave.

622
00:34:36,660 --> 00:34:39,040
Now the distance
between these two

623
00:34:42,030 --> 00:34:45,580
we'd want our QT interval,
which starts from the

624
00:34:45,580 --> 00:34:49,660
beginning of our Q wave
to the end of the T wave

625
00:34:51,980 --> 00:34:55,720
to occur before we
meet the halfway

626
00:34:55,720 --> 00:34:58,100
point between these R waves,

627
00:34:58,500 --> 00:35:01,180
which is relatively
along this line.

628
00:35:01,180 --> 00:35:04,800
I apologize, I am
not the best artist.

629
00:35:07,000 --> 00:35:11,420
In other terms, the
normal QT interval

630
00:35:11,420 --> 00:35:15,240
is between 0.36
to 0.44 seconds.

631
00:35:15,360 --> 00:35:18,640
This can slightly change
between genders, but

632
00:35:18,640 --> 00:35:21,880
is a good reference
point in numbers to know.

633
00:35:22,900 --> 00:35:26,840
A mnemonic to remember
as well as some causes

634
00:35:26,840 --> 00:35:30,120
of a prolonged QT
interval is the mnemonic

635
00:35:30,120 --> 00:35:31,880
as we mentioned in
the previous lecture,

636
00:35:32,140 --> 00:35:34,320
ABCDEF.

637
00:35:34,480 --> 00:35:38,780
We also have congenital
causes of QT prolongation,

638
00:35:38,780 --> 00:35:41,180
such as long QT
syndrome and electrolyte

639
00:35:41,180 --> 00:35:43,880
abnormalities that we
will discuss later on.

640
00:35:49,160 --> 00:35:52,500
So our last step,
step number six,

641
00:35:53,420 --> 00:35:55,800
others, also others
in our mnemonic.

642
00:35:56,360 --> 00:36:02,120
So during assessment of
other things in EKG, we can

643
00:36:02,120 --> 00:36:04,500
look at things like our
SC segment and T waves.

644
00:36:04,500 --> 00:36:07,580
We'll leave it limited to
this at the basic level.

645
00:36:07,920 --> 00:36:12,140
So both changes in either
SC segments or T waves

646
00:36:12,140 --> 00:36:14,760
can indicate some form of
ischemia of heart muscle.

647
00:36:14,780 --> 00:36:16,940
Like we mentioned earlier
in the lecture, we know

648
00:36:16,940 --> 00:36:19,300
that SC depression means
that ischemia is limited

649
00:36:19,300 --> 00:36:22,200
to the sub and the cardio
layer, while SC elevation

650
00:36:22,200 --> 00:36:25,500
is transmural, involving
all layers of the heart.

651
00:36:25,500 --> 00:36:28,760
So in an EKG, we can
see SC segment changes.

652
00:36:29,020 --> 00:36:31,080
Now, like we mentioned before,

653
00:36:31,200 --> 00:36:33,960
although SC segment should
always be isoelectric,

654
00:36:33,960 --> 00:36:39,980
it sometimes will not be,
as is the case in an MI, be

655
00:36:39,980 --> 00:36:43,600
it SC segment elevation
or SC segment depression.

656
00:36:43,620 --> 00:36:46,540
This is why I wanted to
mention earlier on that

657
00:36:46,540 --> 00:36:51,110
the TP segment can be a
great reference point to

658
00:36:51,110 --> 00:36:53,560
use. So just keep that
in the back of your mind.

659
00:36:53,820 --> 00:36:58,720
So for Bohr's purposes,
the T-wave represents

660
00:36:58,720 --> 00:37:01,600
ventricular repolarization,
as we mentioned earlier.

661
00:37:02,320 --> 00:37:05,620
A peak T-wave is a
sign of hyperkalemia,

662
00:37:05,620 --> 00:37:08,140
and a flattened T-wave with a

663
00:37:08,140 --> 00:37:10,560
prominent U-wave is a
sign of hypokalemia.

664
00:37:10,960 --> 00:37:13,360
This makes sense
because if you remember,

665
00:37:13,400 --> 00:37:15,380
potassium is part of
the repolarization

666
00:37:15,380 --> 00:37:18,720
stage, phase 3 of
the action potential.

667
00:37:18,860 --> 00:37:21,240
So if you're affecting
repolarization, this

668
00:37:21,240 --> 00:37:23,880
would be reflected by
changes in the QT interval,

669
00:37:23,940 --> 00:37:26,820
T-wave, and if severe
enough, the QRS complex.

670
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:30,360
So in the clinical setting,
inverted T-waves is one

671
00:37:30,360 --> 00:37:33,640
of the first signs of cardiac
ischemia, making T-waves

672
00:37:33,640 --> 00:37:36,440
an important indicator of
whether or not a person

673
00:37:36,440 --> 00:37:39,440
is experiencing some form
of acute coronary syndrome.

674
00:37:40,880 --> 00:37:43,780
So now, let's try to
interpret this EKG in front of

675
00:37:43,780 --> 00:37:46,240
us using the steps that
we've learned along the way.

676
00:37:46,470 --> 00:37:49,780
So just to reiterate, we
have our orientation, then

677
00:37:49,780 --> 00:37:52,600
rate, rhythm, axis,
intervals, and then others.

678
00:37:52,840 --> 00:37:54,820
So beginning with
step number one,

679
00:37:54,820 --> 00:37:56,880
orientation, we want
to orient ourselves.

680
00:37:57,080 --> 00:37:59,140
Ask ourselves, what
are we looking at?

681
00:37:59,330 --> 00:38:01,620
Are we looking at a
12-lead EKG, rhythm

682
00:38:01,620 --> 00:38:03,080
strips, and what
is the calibration

683
00:38:03,080 --> 00:38:05,320
curve? Is it appropriate?
Is it normal?

684
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:08,660
So looking at this EKG
in front of us, we can

685
00:38:08,660 --> 00:38:12,380
count 12 leads, right?
So we have 1, 2, 3, AVR,

686
00:38:12,380 --> 00:38:16,100
AVL, AVF, V1 to V6, for
a total of 12 leads.

687
00:38:16,580 --> 00:38:20,160
Now to assess the
calibration curve we

688
00:38:20,160 --> 00:38:21,660
see that the calibration
curve in this

689
00:38:21,660 --> 00:38:25,560
EKG is on the left
side of the screen.

690
00:38:26,340 --> 00:38:31,000
So a normal calibration
curve is two big boxes

691
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:35,800
in height and one big
box in width, which is

692
00:38:35,800 --> 00:38:38,620
the calibration curve
that we see here. So now

693
00:38:38,620 --> 00:38:41,260
we know that we're looking
at a 12 lead EKG with

694
00:38:41,260 --> 00:38:43,760
an appropriate, or
normal calibration curve.

695
00:38:43,900 --> 00:38:46,260
Next, we can assess the rate.

696
00:38:46,380 --> 00:38:49,080
So to assess the rate,
we can find any of

697
00:38:49,080 --> 00:38:51,240
our leads. Let's just
look at lead one,

698
00:38:51,460 --> 00:38:54,600
find our QRS complexes.

699
00:38:56,540 --> 00:39:00,120
And in this case, we
can take and assess the

700
00:39:00,120 --> 00:39:04,160
distance between one QRS
complex to the other.

701
00:39:04,400 --> 00:39:07,540
So between these two QRS
complexes, we have about

702
00:39:07,540 --> 00:39:10,440
five big boxes. And
again, between this QRS

703
00:39:10,440 --> 00:39:14,580
complex to the other, we
have about five big boxes.

704
00:39:14,900 --> 00:39:18,380
Now, using one of the
methods we learned

705
00:39:18,380 --> 00:39:21,520
earlier, if we were to
use the number of big

706
00:39:21,520 --> 00:39:24,500
boxes between two
consecutive QRS complexes,

707
00:39:24,680 --> 00:39:31,620
we can do 300 divided by
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, which gives

708
00:39:31,620 --> 00:39:34,700
us a heart rate of approximately
60 beats per minute.

709
00:39:34,720 --> 00:39:37,820
So our rate is approximately
60 beats per minute.

710
00:39:38,140 --> 00:39:39,640
Now the rhythm.

711
00:39:40,040 --> 00:39:41,140
Next step.

712
00:39:41,260 --> 00:39:45,000
So to assess the rhythm,
first we want to locate

713
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:48,600
or identify if we have any
P waves. Now the P waves,

714
00:39:48,600 --> 00:39:51,200
which should precede
each QRS complex, can be

715
00:39:51,200 --> 00:39:54,060
found in any of the leads,
but particularly there are

716
00:39:54,060 --> 00:39:56,640
some leads that are, as
Dr. Collins referred to

717
00:39:56,640 --> 00:39:59,700
them, the money leads. Now
the money leads are the

